# duty.claim — <function> <statement-kind> <check that its arm must call>
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE A CLAIM-FREE RULE PROVABLY CANNOT DO THIS, and the
# proof is a measurement. Commit 756aed65 added the missing call
# `field_borrow_conflicts` to visit_stmt's Assign arm. On the revision BEFORE it,
# over the statement domain:
#
#     before:  NO statement arm calls field_borrow_conflicts anywhere.  Zero.
#     after:   exactly one — visit_stmt / Assign.
#
# So "the siblings of one switch are their own specification" cannot reach this
# defect: before the fix no sibling was doing the right thing to learn from. The
# knowledge that an ASSIGNMENT is a write, and that a write owes the conflict
# check, lives nowhere in the code until someone writes the call.
#
# ⚠ THE FUNCTION IS PART OF THE CLAIM, and the first version left it out. Stating
# only (kind, check) made the rule demand the borrow checker's duties of every
# function that dispatches over statement kinds — gen_stmt (codegen), subst_stmt
# (monomorphisation), scan_uses_stmt (a use scanner), nine of them, none of which
# owes anything of the sort. 72 rows, 64 of them meaningless. A duty attaches to
# a ROLE; naming the role is the claim.
#
# ⚠ AND ITS OWN CROSS-CHECK SAID SO. `duty_never_honoured` reported 7 of 8
# claimed duties honoured NOWHERE — far likelier a bad claim than a defect at
# every site. It was left firing rather than trimmed to look right, and this file
# is the result of believing it.
#
#   FALSIFIABLE, BY CONSTRUCTION: every row below is honoured somewhere today
#   except where a defect is named. If a row stops being honoured anywhere, the
#   claim has drifted from the code and duty_never_honoured says so.

# ── visit_stmt: the borrow checker's statement dispatcher ───────────────────
# A WRITE THROUGH A PLACE records its loans. All seven place-write spellings do
# this today, so these rows yield NOTHING — they are a RATCHET: the eighth
# spelling somebody adds will fire here instead of being found a year later.
visit_stmt  FieldWrite       place_write_loans
visit_stmt  IndexWrite       place_write_loans
visit_stmt  FieldIndexWrite  place_write_loans
visit_stmt  DerefWrite       place_write_loans
visit_stmt  DerefFieldWrite  place_write_loans
visit_stmt  TupleWrite       place_write_loans
visit_stmt  ChainFieldWrite  place_write_loans

# A WRITE CHECKS ITS ROOT IS USABLE. Six of the seven do. DerefWrite does not,
# and that is a CONFIRMED LIVE HOLE with a proven one-variable repro:
# `*r = S{…}` compiles while `r.f = …` refuses, both with `&*r` live.
# See docs/baghunt/open_derefwrite_shared_borrow.md — and note that
# `check_live` is NOT the repair, only the symptom's neighbour.
visit_stmt  FieldWrite       check_live
visit_stmt  IndexWrite       check_live
visit_stmt  FieldIndexWrite  check_live
visit_stmt  DerefWrite       check_live
visit_stmt  DerefFieldWrite  check_live
visit_stmt  TupleWrite       check_live
visit_stmt  ChainFieldWrite  check_live

# THE ORIGINAL. `x = v` is a whole-binding assignment rather than a place write,
# so it takes neither of the two above; what it owes is the conflict check, and
# for four rounds it did not ask. This row is the known answer selftest.sh
# checks in both directions across 756aed65.
visit_stmt  Assign           field_borrow_conflicts
